/* V68 — the armoury finally owns the mechanic the game is built on.

   Two things have been true at once and should not have been. Play feedback asked, in as many words,
   that switching weapons feel like a real difference and that an advanced weapon kills more. And V63
   made the blast the centre of the game: what a tap does now is detonate, and how much of a formation
   goes depends on how far that detonation reaches. Those two facts had nothing to do with each other.
   Every weapon produced exactly the same blast, so the most consequential number in the arena was the
   one number no purchase could touch.

   The weapon stats already carried the vocabulary for this and were spending it on side effects. A
   scatter volley has `splash`, a nova cannon has `aoe`, an arc chain has `chain` — three different
   words for "this weapon works on more than one thing at a time", each wired to its own small rate-
   limited extra. They map onto the blast directly:

   The figures below are measured: one tap on the best point of a four-hostile formation, same board
   for every weapon, weapons at level five.

     PULSE DRIVER    baseline reach. Three of four on a row, a wedge and a column; the whole
                     keystone. Its levels buy damage, so its answer to an armoured hostile is to hit
                     it hard rather than to reach further.
     SCATTER VOLLEY  a wider blast, growing with its splash stat. Four of four on everything by level
                     five. The crowd weapon widens the thing that clears crowds.
     VOID LANCE      a blast that takes one neighbour instead of everything in reach: two of four on
                     every shape, always. A piercing lance is not an area weapon; it trades the
                     cascade for damage that opens armour in a single tap, and plays formations by
                     killing the hard member directly rather than by breaking the shape.
     ARC CHAIN       extra links and a gentler falloff, so its cascades keep travelling instead of
                     dying out. Four of four on every shape from level one -- including the column,
                     which nothing else clears until it is fully upgraded. Reach is unchanged: it is
                     not a bigger explosion, it is a longer one.
     SHARD SIPHON    baseline, identical to pulse. It is the economy weapon and buys credits.
     NOVA CANNON     much wider, with a steep falloff: everything within reach at once and then
                     nothing. The shape-deleter, at the cost of a long recharge.

   Crucially the multipliers apply *at detonation*, not to `V63.radius` itself. V64 spaces formations
   against that constant, so if the weapon moved it the shapes would grow with the blast and nothing
   would change. The arena stays fixed; what varies is how much of it a given weapon can take.

   Where a weapon has an area stat, its blast is derived from that stat and therefore grows as the
   weapon is levelled -- buying an upgrade visibly changes how formations come apart, which is what
   "an advanced weapon kills more" should mean in a game whose verb is an area rather than another
   point of damage. The three weapons with no area stat (pulse, lance, siphon) are flat by design:
   their levels buy damage and credits, and giving them creeping reach as well would collapse the
   whole set back into one profile. */

const V68={
 /* Reach per point of `splash`. Scatter runs 78 to 158, giving roughly 1.19x to 1.38x. */
 splash:1/420,
 /* Reach per point of `aoe`. Nova runs 70 to 170, giving roughly 1.32x to 1.77x -- wide enough to
    take a whole row in one tap, which is the fantasy the weapon is sold on. */
 aoe:1/220,
 /* What a heavy single-target weapon gives up: its blast takes one neighbour instead of everything
    in reach.

    The first version shrank its radius to 0.78x instead, and measured, that removed the lance's
    chain completely -- 1 of 4 on a row, a wedge and a column, against 3 or 4 for everything else.
    The reason is structural rather than a bad number: V64 spaces formations at 92% of the blast's
    reach, so any radius cut past about 8% drops it under one gap and the chain stops existing.
    Radius is close to a binary switch at this spacing; a cap on victims is an actual dial. The lance
    keeps full reach and takes the one hostile nearest the kill, which is also a better description
    of what a piercing weapon is. */
 lanceTargets:1,
 /* Nova's blast is enormous, so its falloff is steepened to compensate: it takes everything within
    reach at once and the second link goes nowhere. Without this a nova tap on a column would clear
    the entire arena, which is not a decision, it is a button. */
 novaFalloff:.72,
 /* Arc's gentler falloff.

    Set to 1.12 first, and measured, that bought nothing at all: arc cleared exactly as much of every
    shape as the starter weapon. The extra links it pays for were unreachable, because reach runs out
    before the depth cap does. At V64's one-gap spacing a second link needs to retain about 87% of
    the first blast's radius to connect, and 0.78 x 1.12 retains 87.4% of the radius but only 96% of
    the *catch distance* once the target's own body is accounted for -- just under. A depth bonus on
    top of a falloff that cannot reach the next hostile is a stat that does nothing.

    At 1.22 the second link connects and the third is marginal, so arc is the one weapon whose
    cascades keep travelling. */
 arcFalloff:1.22
};

/* Keyed by weapon id rather than inferred from the stats.

   Inferring was the first attempt and it was wrong in a way worth recording, because it read as the
   more elegant option: "a weapon with damage of 2 or more and no area stat is a heavy single-target
   weapon, so it trades reach for punch". That is a true sentence about the lance and a false one
   about half the armoury. Pulse gains a point of damage every two levels and the siphon every three,
   so an upgraded starter weapon and an upgraded economy weapon both matched the rule and both quietly
   lost 22% of their blast as a reward for being levelled up. A rule that fires on a coincidence
   between two stats will keep finding new coincidences; the id is what actually identifies a
   weapon. */
const V68_BLAST={
 pulse:()=>({radius:1,depth:0,falloff:1,max:Infinity}),
 scatter:(c)=>({radius:1+c.splash*V68.splash,depth:0,falloff:1,max:Infinity}),
 lance:()=>({radius:1,depth:0,falloff:1,max:V68.lanceTargets}),
 arc:(c)=>({
  radius:1,
  max:Infinity,
  /* One extra link per two points of chain, capped by V63's own ceiling. Arc runs 2 to 6, so it buys
     one link at first and two once upgraded. */
  depth:Math.min(V63.depthMax,Math.floor(c.chain/2)),
  falloff:V68.arcFalloff
 }),
 siphon:()=>({radius:1,depth:0,falloff:1,max:Infinity}),
 nova:(c)=>({radius:1+c.aoe*V68.aoe,depth:0,falloff:V68.novaFalloff,max:Infinity})
};

/* The equipped id at the moment the run started, captured alongside the stats the run is using.
   `v14Save.weapon` could be read live, but it can change in the armoury mid-session and a run should
   fight with the weapon it began with. */
const v68Begin=begin;
begin=function(mode='solo',seed){
 const out=v68Begin(mode,seed);
 game.v68Weapon=(typeof v14Save==='object'&&v14Save)?v14Save.weapon:null;
 return out;
};

v63Power=function(){
 const c=game.v14Combat;
 if(!c||typeof c!=='object')return{radius:1,depth:0,falloff:1,max:Infinity};
 const profile=V68_BLAST[game.v68Weapon];
 if(profile)return profile(c);
 /* A weapon this table has never heard of still gets something sensible from whichever area stat it
    carries, rather than silently falling back to baseline. */
 return{
  radius:1+(c.splash>0?c.splash*V68.splash:0)+(c.aoe>0?c.aoe*V68.aoe:0),
  depth:c.chain>0?Math.min(V63.depthMax,Math.floor(c.chain/2)):0,
  falloff:c.aoe>0?V68.novaFalloff:c.chain>0?V68.arcFalloff:1,
  max:Infinity
 };
};

/* ---- Saying so ----

   The armoury descriptions were written before the blast existed and describe a game where a weapon
   is a damage profile. They are rewritten from the same table that drives the mechanic, so the copy
   cannot drift away from what the weapon actually does -- a shop that lies about a stat is worse
   than a shop that omits it. */

const V68_BLURB={
 pulse:'Dependable single-target driver. Baseline blast; levels add raw punch and titan damage.',
 scatter:'Wide blast radius — takes more of a formation with every kill. Built for crowds.',
 lance:'Heavy piercing lance. Opens armour in one tap; its blast takes one hostile, never a whole formation.',
 arc:'Its cascades run further: extra chain links and almost no falloff between them. Cuts columns apart.',
 siphon:'Converts kills into credits. Baseline blast, far better stage payouts.',
 nova:'Enormous blast, then nothing — one tap takes a whole formation. Long recharge.'
};

for(const weapon of v14Weapons){
 if(V68_BLURB[weapon.id])weapon.desc=V68_BLURB[weapon.id];
}
